REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w03","title":"Vocabulary, modal verbs, phrases and idioms","summary":"What it means to know a word (synonyms and antonyms, homophones and homonyms, collocation, and how words change form in a sentence), the modal verbs of English and what each one does, and sixty common phrases and idioms with their meanings and the lectures' example sentences.","n":3,"lectures":[{"code":"3.1","title":"What is good vocabulary - part 1","video":"A2eI-Ttmd7I","seconds":1825},{"code":"3.2","title":"What is good vocabulary - part 2","video":"pHkc23_0ULI","seconds":1748},{"code":"3.3","title":"What is good vocabulary - part 3","video":"b8Wq_9P7uqI","seconds":1732},{"code":"3.4","title":"Modal verbs - a quick review","video":"XFPZfdWCom4","seconds":1794},{"code":"3.5","title":"Phrases and Idioms - 1","video":"-aTwRZVfo64","seconds":1301},{"code":"3.6","title":"Phrases & idioms - part 2","video":"r5wn7XuDCV8","seconds":1159}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-is-vocabulary","level":2,"title":"What is vocabulary?"},{"id":"what-we-know-about-a-word","level":3,"title":"What we know about a word"},{"id":"five-signs-of-a-good-vocabulary","level":2,"title":"Five signs of a good vocabulary"},{"id":"synonyms-and-antonyms","level":2,"title":"Synonyms and antonyms"},{"id":"dictionary-and-thesaurus","level":2,"title":"Dictionary and thesaurus"},{"id":"homophones-and-homonyms","level":2,"title":"Homophones and homonyms"},{"id":"collocation","level":2,"title":"Collocation"},{"id":"adjectives-and-their-nouns","level":3,"title":"Adjectives and their nouns"},{"id":"verbs-nouns-and-adverbs","level":3,"title":"Verbs, nouns and adverbs"},{"id":"how-words-change-in-a-sentence","level":2,"title":"How words change in a sentence"},{"id":"two-kinds-of-change","level":3,"title":"Two kinds of change"},{"id":"how-to-build-your-vocabulary","level":2,"title":"How to build your vocabulary"},{"id":"modal-verbs","level":2,"title":"Modal verbs"},{"id":"what-all-modals-share","level":3,"title":"What all modals share"},{"id":"the-modals-one-by-one","level":3,"title":"The modals one by one"},{"id":"phrases-that-work-like-modals","level":3,"title":"Phrases that work like modals"},{"id":"phrases-and-idioms","level":2,"title":"Phrases and idioms"},{"id":"common-phrases-from-lecture-3-5","level":2,"title":"Common phrases from lecture 3.5"},{"id":"phrases-1-to-10","level":3,"title":"Phrases 1 to 10"},{"id":"phrases-11-to-20","level":3,"title":"Phrases 11 to 20"},{"id":"phrases-21-to-30","level":3,"title":"Phrases 21 to 30"},{"id":"common-phrases-from-lecture-3-6","level":2,"title":"Common phrases from lecture 3.6"},{"id":"phrases-31-to-40","level":3,"title":"Phrases 31 to 40"},{"id":"phrases-41-to-50","level":3,"title":"Phrases 41 to 50"},{"id":"phrases-51-to-60","level":3,"title":"Phrases 51 to 60"},{"id":"phrases-that-look-alike","level":2,"title":"Phrases that look alike"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vocabulary</strong> (3.1): all the words you know <strong>and</strong> all you know about each word: its meaning, spelling, pronunciation, part of speech, its forms, and which words it goes with. Nobody knows exactly how many words they know; a safe guess for a college graduate is 30,000 to 50,000.</li>\n<li><strong>Five signs of a good vocabulary</strong> (3.1): the number and variety of words you know, similar and opposite words, how each word goes with other words, how new words are made from old ones, and how each word is used in a sentence.</li>\n<li><strong>Synonyms and antonyms</strong> (3.1, 3.2): words that mean almost the same (<em>road, avenue, lane, street</em>) and words that mean the opposite (<em>friend, enemy</em>). A <strong>thesaurus</strong> groups words by meaning; a <strong>dictionary</strong> lists them in alphabetical order.</li>\n<li><strong>Homophones and homonyms</strong> (3.2): <em>right</em> and <em>write</em> sound the same but differ in spelling and meaning (homophones). <em>Beat</em> (to hit) and <em>beat</em> (a policeman’s area) are one spelling with two meanings (homonyms).</li>\n<li><strong>Collocation</strong> (3.2, 3.3): it is fixed which words go together: <em>many students</em> but <em>much water</em>; <em>few</em> with things we can count, <em>little</em> with things we cannot; <em>electric guitar</em> but <em>electrical engineer</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflection and derivation</strong> (3.3): in a sentence, words often change form. If the part of speech stays the same (<em>week → weeks</em>, <em>come → came</em>), it is <strong>inflection</strong>. If it changes (<em>politics → political</em>, <em>acquire → acquisition</em>), it is <strong>derivation</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Modal verbs</strong> (3.4): <em>can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would, must, ought to, need</em>. They stand before the main verb, which keeps its bare form (<em>I can run</em>), and they add <strong>modality</strong>: ability, permission, request, possibility, advice, obligation. <em>Have to, be able to, be allowed to</em> and <em>used to</em> work like them.</li>\n<li><strong>Phrases and idioms</strong> (3.5, 3.6): an <strong>idiom</strong> means something different from its words (<em>break a leg</em> = good luck). The two lectures teach 60 common phrases, from <em>a dark horse</em> to <em>it’s all Greek to me</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-vocabulary\">What is vocabulary?</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>People often say that someone “has a very good vocabulary”. What do they mean? That the person knows <strong>many</strong> words? Or that the person can <strong>use</strong> the words they know creatively, in many ways? Lecture 3.1 starts with Shakespeare: people say he knew fewer than 30,000 words when English had nearly 100,000. Yet he wrote so well that he is still popular today (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). So a good vocabulary is not just a big number.</p>\n<p>Words are the first thing we learn in a language: first words, then phrases, then sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Vocabulary</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Vocabulary</strong> is all the words we know, <strong>and</strong> all that we know <strong>about</strong> those words (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). The lecturer’s picture: a box, a bag or an <em>almirah</em> (cupboard) that holds only words.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-we-know-about-a-word\">What we know about a word</h3>\n<p>Take the word <strong>cat</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). We know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>its <strong>meaning</strong>;</li>\n<li>its <strong>spelling</strong>;</li>\n<li>its <strong>pronunciation</strong> (how it is said);</li>\n<li>its <strong>category</strong>: its part of speech, such as noun, pronoun, verb, adjective or adverb (parts of speech are in <a href=\"#/english1/w02\">Week 2</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Take the verb <strong>read</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). We also know how its form changes in a sentence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One person: <em>John reads the Bible every morning. Mary reads her email every few hours in her office.</em> We add <strong>-s</strong>.</li>\n<li>Two people: <em>John and Mary read their Bible in the church on Sundays.</em> No -s.</li>\n<li>Past forms (here the form after <em>have</em>): <em>I have read all of Shakespeare.</em> The spelling stays <em>read</em>; only the pronunciation changes (it sounds like <em>red</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We also know how to make other words from it: <em>read → reader</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). And we know that a <em>Reader</em> at a university is a teaching position there (“John is a Reader in a university”), a meaning that has nothing to do with reading, though the spelling is the same.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">How many words do we know?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Many people know only one language, some others know two, three, four or even more languages, but no one knows exactly how many words they know” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). Only rough guesses exist:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a child of five knows about <strong>5,000</strong> words;</li>\n<li>a college graduate: a safe guess is <strong>30,000 to 50,000</strong> words;</li>\n<li>people who read, write or teach for a living, or lawyers, may know <strong>more than 50,000</strong> words in the language of their work (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>A <strong>monolingual</strong> is a person who knows only one language. There is nothing bad about it; it depends on where you grow up. In many communities of southern India (Kerala, coastal Karnataka, Goa, the Tuticorin and Rameswaram side) people know at least three or four languages (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n<p>People also differ in how many words they know, even in one family (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). The slide: “Some people understand nearly all the words they see and hear. Some others understand few words they see and hear. Similarly, some people can speak and write many different kinds of words; some others, on the other hand, can speak and write relatively few words correctly.”</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A small vocabulary is not a handicap</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Knowing fewer words does not mean your <strong>world</strong> is smaller. With those very words, people do everything that someone with a large vocabulary does, because words are flexible: they take new meanings and stretch (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"five-signs-of-a-good-vocabulary\">Five signs of a good vocabulary</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">What makes a vocabulary good</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“We can say somebody’s vocabulary is good or bad depending upon their knowledge of the following” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Number &amp; variety of words</li>\n<li>Number of similar &amp; different words</li>\n<li>The way each word goes with other words</li>\n<li>The way new words are made out of old words</li>\n<li>The way each word is used in a sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sign</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Number and variety</td>\n<td>not only <em>teacher</em> but <em>professor, instructor, preceptor</em>; not only <em>doctor</em> but <em>healer</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Similar and different words</td>\n<td><em>road, avenue, lane</em> (similar); <em>friend, enemy</em> (opposite)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Which word goes with which</td>\n<td>you <em>drink</em> water but <em>eat</em> rice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. New words from old</td>\n<td><em>person → personal, personify, personality</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Use in a sentence</td>\n<td><em>John reads books</em> (one person, so <em>-s</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Signs 1 and 2 are the next section. Sign 3 is <strong>collocation</strong> and sign 4 is <strong>derivation</strong>, both explained in full later on this page. Here is what lecture 3.1 says about signs 3 to 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Sign 3: which word goes with which</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). <em>Drink</em> is followed by <em>water</em>, not by <em>rice</em>. In English you <strong>eat</strong> solids, <strong>drink</strong> liquids and <strong>breathe</strong> gases. Other languages differ: in Bangla you can “eat” rice, water and even air, and in Hindi people idiomatically “eat” and even “drink” air.</p>\n<p>Small words have partners too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). <em>The</em> is usually followed by a noun. <em>An</em> is usually followed by a word whose spelling starts with a, e, i, o or u.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>an MLA</em>, <em>an MP</em>: the spelling starts with M, but the letter M is said “em”, which starts with a vowel <strong>sound</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>an elegant house</em>: <em>an</em> comes before <em>elegant</em> (it starts with e), even though the noun is <em>house</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“It is not that any word can go with any word; then we will have chaos” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Sign 4: new words from old</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). From <em>person</em> we make <em>personal, personalize, personify, personality, personification</em>: all made “by adding things to a word called person”.</p>\n<p><strong>Sign 5: use in a sentence</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). <em>John reads books. Mary treats people</em> (she is a doctor; she cures people). John and Mary are one person each, so <em>read</em> and <em>treat</em> take <strong>-s</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"synonyms-and-antonyms\">Synonyms and antonyms</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Number and variety of words” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>): “Nearly all know words for their <strong>mother and father</strong>; but some also know that they are called <strong>parents</strong>. Nearly all know words for <strong>brother and sister</strong>; but some also know that they are called <strong>siblings</strong>. In all parts of life, some people know only essential words, but some others know more than essential words.”</p>\n<p>Food is another example (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>). Many of us know <em>food</em>. Some also know <em>meal, breakfast, lunch, dinner, supper, snacks, cookies, savouries, sweets, fries</em>. Those who know more words than only <em>food</em>, more than only what is necessary, have a better vocabulary.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Words for “friends” (the lecture’s extract at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The extract, from R. K. Narayan’s novel <em>Swami and Friends</em>: “At first, Swaminathan was uncertain of his happiness in the new school. But he excited the curiosity that all new comers do, and found himself to his great satisfaction the centre of attraction to Second C. All his new classmates, remarkably new faces, often clustered round him to see him and hear him talk. He had not yet picked the few he would have liked to call his chums. He still believed that his Albert Mission set was intact.”</p>\n<p>The question: how many words here mean nearly the same as <em>friends</em>?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Second C</strong>: the name of a class (a section), but here it means the boys in it, his new group.</li>\n<li><strong>classmates</strong>: the boys in his class.</li>\n<li><strong>faces</strong>: faces do not exist without people, so <em>new faces</em> means new people around him.</li>\n<li><strong>chums</strong>: friends so close that you share secrets with them.</li>\n<li><strong>set</strong>: a group of friends (<em>his Albert Mission set</em> = his friends from his old school).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> five: <em>Second C, classmates, faces, chums, set</em>. “One who can write or understand these words has a better vocabulary than another who cannot” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Synonyms</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Synonyms</strong> are words that mean <strong>almost alike</strong> (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Road</em>: avenue, alley, lane, by-lane, path, street, thoroughfare, way (as in <em>subway</em>). They do not mean exactly alike (an alley is not a lane, an avenue is not a street), but all are public ways on which people can walk or drive freely. (A <em>thoroughfare</em> is a public road.)</li>\n<li><em>Home</em>: abode, apartment, bungalow, flat, house, nest, residence, villa. <em>I’m going home. I’m going to my flat. I’m going to my apartment.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</li>\n<li><em>Good</em>: fine, nice, excellent, marvellous, outstanding, extraordinary, splendid (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Antonyms</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Antonyms</strong> are words that mean the <strong>opposite</strong> (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>night / day</em>, <em>dark / bright</em>, <em>poor / rich</em>, <em>come / go</em>, <em>big / small</em>, <em>quick / slow</em>, <em>high / low</em>, <em>light / dark</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Friend</em>: competitor, contestant, critic, enemy, foe, rival. These are antonyms of <em>friend</em>, but <strong>synonyms of one another</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>Good</em>: bad, stinking, rotten, lousy, filthy, ugly, dirty, low (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). (Somehow, <em>high</em> goes with good and <em>low</em> with bad, though this is not logical.)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Functional and good vocabulary</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“If one knows only <em>friends</em> and <em>enemies</em>, then one can be said to have only a <strong>functional vocabulary</strong>. But if one knows their antonyms and synonyms as well, then one can be said to have a <strong>good vocabulary</strong>” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>). <em>Functional</em> = enough to get the job done.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why does every language have so many words for one thing? Things in the world come in many shades, shapes and sizes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). There may be a <strong>generic term</strong> (a general word for the whole class), such as <em>basket</em>, and then words for kinds of it: a <em>case</em> and a smaller <em>cassette</em> do the same job. With such words we show <strong>shades of meaning</strong>. And because the world has light and darkness, rich and poor, languages also have antonyms (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n<p>Lecture 3.2 asked for synonyms of five words; here are the answers from its slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Synonyms on the slide</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>come</td>\n<td>arrive, join, reach, report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>go</td>\n<td>depart, exit, leave, march</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>merchant</td>\n<td>businessman, dealer, salesman, trader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>big</td>\n<td>enormous, gigantic, huge, large</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>quick</td>\n<td>express, fast, immediate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture also gives <em>great, overwhelming, mountainous</em> for <em>big</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>), <em>at once</em> for <em>quick</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>), and <em>manufacture, create, design, come up with</em> for <em>make</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). The lecture’s sentences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Ruth came to Chennai last month</em> or <em>Ruth joined Chennai last month</em>: the shade of meaning changes only slightly (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). (In standard English you <em>join</em> a group or a workplace: <em>Ruth joined the Chennai office</em>.)</li>\n<li><em>The plane goes from here to Calcutta at 12:30 hours</em> or <em>departs</em>, <em>exits</em>, <em>leaves</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). (The lecturer warns that some of these sound too formal; <em>departs</em> and <em>leaves</em> sound natural here, <em>exits</em> does not.)</li>\n<li><em>I know a merchant who sold me a pen</em> or <em>a businessman who sold me a pen</em>. Flipkart and Amazon are merchants: business companies, dealers, traders (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Synonyms are never exactly alike</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Good</em> can often be replaced by <em>fine, nice, excellent, marvellous, wonderful</em>, but each has its own place (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>fine</strong> for thinness: <em>Fine slices were cut</em> (razor-thin slices).</li>\n<li><strong>nice</strong> for a smell: <em>It is smelling nice.</em> “Smelling fine” is possible, but it is not the same thing.</li>\n<li><strong>excellent</strong> when someone <strong>excels</strong>, does more than expected: a student told to write 10 pages writes 11, 12 or 13. That work is excellent.</li>\n<li><strong>marvellous</strong> or <strong>wonderful</strong> for something that makes you marvel or wonder: a five- or six-year-old child who can spell every word in the dictionary.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Good writers use “only one particular word for that context, because that is the best word that would fit there” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">One word or many?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If you have only one word for each thing, you can still use the language, but you are at the beginner level (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). Young children know <em>papa</em> and <em>mummy</em>; growing up, they learn <em>parents</em>, <em>grandparents</em>, <em>siblings</em>. “This extension, this extra thing, brings power to your vocabulary.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Some antonyms are made with a prefix</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s thesaurus slide lists <em>disagree</em> among the antonyms of <em>agree</em>: <em>dis-</em> + <em>agree</em>. A <strong>prefix</strong> is a small part added at the start of a word. Other common ones (my examples): <em>happy → unhappy</em>, <em>possible → impossible</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The lecture’s activity at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>: find about five synonyms each of <em>ask, bad, good, student, teacher</em>.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The answers on the lecture’s slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ask</strong>: ascertain, enquire, examine, get, investigate, question, tell</li>\n<li><strong>bad</strong>: dirty, evil, harmful, ill, low, poor, unwell</li>\n<li><strong>good</strong>: agreeable, desirable, excellent, fine, great, marvellous, outstanding, splendid, terrific, wonderful</li>\n<li><strong>student</strong>: boy/girl, lad/lass, disciple, intern, learner, pupil</li>\n<li><strong>teacher</strong>: guide, guru, instructor, master, preceptor, professor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Check your own list with a thesaurus or a dictionary.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"dictionary-and-thesaurus\">Dictionary and thesaurus</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Dictionary</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>dictionary</strong> arranges words <strong>alphabetically</strong>: words with <em>a</em> come before words with <em>b</em>, and <em>ca-</em> comes before <em>ce-</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). For each word it gives the spelling, meaning, category (part of speech), usage and pronunciation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Thesaurus</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>thesaurus</strong> groups words <strong>according to meaning</strong> (semantically). All the words that mean <em>good</em> are together: <em>fine, nice, excellent, marvellous, splendid, outstanding</em>. All the words that mean <em>bad</em> are together: <em>bad, evil, low, lowly, dirty, foul</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide: a thesaurus “will help you use accurate words”. It helps you see the difference between words like <em>accept, acknowledge, admit, agree, approve, assent, consent</em>, and their antonyms like <em>differ, disagree, dissent</em>. The slide shows <em>Roget’s Thesaurus</em> and the <em>Oxford Advanced Learner’s Dictionary</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Keep both at hand</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Anyone who wants to be a good writer or speaker should keep a dictionary <strong>and</strong> a thesaurus on the table: while preparing a talk, writing an article or a paper (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). Soft copies on your phone or laptop are fine.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"homophones-and-homonyms\">Homophones and homonyms</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Words are related by form” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). Some words share their <strong>sound</strong>, some share their <strong>spelling</strong> too, and yet they mean different things. Someone with a good vocabulary knows that the same word can mean different things in different contexts, and is a noun in one and a verb in another (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Homophones</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Homophones</strong> are words that <strong>sound alike</strong> but mean different things; they are often spelt differently (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>right</em> (r-i-g-h-t) and <em>write</em> (w-r-i-t-e): the same sound, different spellings and meanings.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Homonyms</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Homonyms</strong> are words with the <strong>same spelling</strong> and the <strong>same sound</strong> but <strong>different meanings</strong>, often as different parts of speech (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). Lecture 3.3 lists them with synonyms, antonyms and homophones as part of a good vocabulary.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Beat</em>, verb and noun (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>beat</strong> as a verb = to assault (to hit). <em>The culprit was badly beaten by the crowd.</em></li>\n<li>A metaphorical use of the verb: <em>The Indian team was badly beaten by the visitors.</em> (They lost the match.)</li>\n<li><strong>beat</strong> as a noun = a domain, an area someone covers. <em>The policeman was on his beat.</em> A newspaper reporter may cover only events at IIT Madras: IIT Madras is that reporter’s beat (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>beat</strong> as a noun = a rhythm, as on a drum (a percussion instrument), or a <em>heartbeat</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> one spelling and one sound, but different meanings and different parts of speech: <em>beat</em> is a homonym.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s homonyms, from its slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>One meaning</th>\n<th>Another meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>count</td>\n<td>(verb) to enumerate, to count</td>\n<td>(noun) a kind of landlord with a certain position (a nobleman)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>deal</td>\n<td>(verb) to do business with</td>\n<td>(noun) a purchase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>board</td>\n<td>(noun) a group of managers</td>\n<td>(verb) to climb onto: <em>board a train, a bus, a plane, a ship</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>express</td>\n<td>(verb) to speak or write</td>\n<td>(adjective) fast, as in an <em>express</em> train</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>interest</td>\n<td>(noun) a charge on a loan</td>\n<td>(verb) to attract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>light</td>\n<td>(noun) the opposite of dark</td>\n<td>(adjective) the opposite of heavy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>miss</td>\n<td>(noun) a title before an unmarried woman’s name</td>\n<td>(verb) to be late and not get something: <em>a missed call</em>, <em>to miss a train</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>part</td>\n<td>(noun) an organ, a piece</td>\n<td>(verb) to leave</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>party</td>\n<td>(noun) a group, generally of like-minded politicians</td>\n<td>(noun or verb) a celebration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>press</td>\n<td>(noun) an outfit for printing a journal</td>\n<td>(verb) to apply force</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>right</td>\n<td>(adjective) correct; the opposite of left</td>\n<td>(noun) the opposite of duty, as in <em>rights and duties</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rest</td>\n<td>(noun) all that remains in a group</td>\n<td>(verb) to be in a state of no motion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>try</td>\n<td>(noun) an effort, an attempt</td>\n<td>(verb) to test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vest</td>\n<td>(noun) an undergarment</td>\n<td>(verb) to give, as in <em>vested power</em>, <em>vested interest</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Homophone or homonym?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Homophone</strong>: only the <strong>sound</strong> is shared (<em>right / write</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Homonym</strong>: the spelling <strong>and</strong> the sound are shared (<em>beat / beat</em>, <em>board / board</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tip: <em>-phone</em> means sound (as in <em>telephone</em>), so a homo<strong>phone</strong> is about sound.</p>\n</div></section>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Homophone or homonym? (a) <em>right</em> and <em>write</em> (b) <em>light</em> (not dark) and <em>light</em> (not heavy) (c) <em>deal</em> (do business) and <em>deal</em> (a purchase)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a) <strong>Homophones</strong>: the same sound, but different spellings and meanings.\n(b) <strong>Homonyms</strong>: the same spelling and sound; one is a noun, the other an adjective.\n(c) <strong>Homonyms</strong>: the same spelling and sound; one is a verb, the other a noun.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"collocation\">Collocation</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Do you ever stand on a road and say just “good”? No, unless you are doing a magic show and want to attract people (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). We do not use words in isolation. In the lecture’s examples <em>good</em> (an adjective) is followed by a noun: <em>good morning, good evening, good day, a good cup of tea, a good man, a good woman, a good friend, a good student, a good boy, a good girl</em>. Since we always use words together, rules fix <strong>which words go with which</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Collocation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“For every word it is fixed which words it can follow, and which other words it can be followed by. This relationship among words is called <strong>collocation</strong>” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n<p>In other words, collocation is the constraint (the limit), the relationship, the rule that decides which word can follow or come before which word (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). Lecture 3.3 puts it in one line: “How any word can relate to other words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“Fewer people make mistakes with synonyms and antonyms than with collocation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). The lecture’s first example is the <strong>quantifiers</strong> (adjectives of quantity) <em>many, much, few, little</em>. They depend on whether a noun is <strong>countable</strong> (we can count it: one boy, two boys; it has a plural) or <strong>uncountable</strong> (we cannot count it one by one: water, milk; it has no plural).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Countable nouns: <em>boys, girls, students</em></th>\n<th>Uncountable nouns: <em>water, milk, tea, sugar, air</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a large quantity</td>\n<td><strong>many</strong> students</td>\n<td><strong>much</strong> water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a small quantity</td>\n<td><strong>few</strong> boys</td>\n<td><strong>little</strong> water</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>You cannot say <em>much boys</em> or <em>much students</em>; say <em>many students</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</li>\n<li>You cannot say <em>many water, many milk, many tea, many sugar, many air</em>; say <em>much milk, much water</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Few</em> goes only with countable nouns: not <em>few water, few milk, few coffee</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Little</em> goes only with uncountable nouns: <em>little water, little milk, little coffee, little air</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture’s sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>): <em>Chennai has little water for so many people.</em> Not <em>few water</em>: water cannot be counted.</p>\n<h3 id=\"adjectives-and-their-nouns\">Adjectives and their nouns</h3>\n<p>The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Adjective</th>\n<th>Goes with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>electric</td>\n<td>light, guitar, line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>electrical</td>\n<td>engineer, energy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cold</td>\n<td>day, sweat, wave, shoulder, drink</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Not <em>electric engineer</em>: it is an <em>electrical engineer</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Cold</em> goes wherever temperature can come, really or metaphorically: <em>cold day, cold sweat, cold wave, cold place, cold mountain, cold river, cold sea</em>, and also <em>cold welcome, cold reception</em> (an unfriendly one). <em>Cold book, cold camera, cold tree</em> are not usual (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Clouds can be <em>thick, black, grey, silver, red</em> or <em>white</em>, and clouds <em>float</em>: <em>floating clouds</em>, not <em>running</em> or <em>flowing clouds</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Water <em>flows</em>: <em>flowing water, flowing river, flowing stream</em>; but not <em>floating water</em>. Boats float: <em>a floating boat, a floating yacht</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"verbs-nouns-and-adverbs\">Verbs, nouns and adverbs</h3>\n<p>Lecture 3.3 adds more pairs (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A verb is generally followed by a noun, but not by any noun. <strong>Drink</strong> takes liquid nouns: <em>drink milk, drink water, drink tea, drink coffee</em>. <strong>Eat</strong> takes solid nouns: <em>eat pudding, biscuits, cake, mutton, fish, rice, vegetables</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Adverbs</strong> describe verbs: how did he walk? <em>He walked slowly</em> or <em>fast</em>, <em>happily</em> or <em>sadly</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li>A noun can be described before or after: <em>a college of commerce</em> or <em>a commerce college</em>; <em>a college of engineering</em> or <em>an engineering college</em>. Then comes a verb: <em>The college teaches higher skills</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“Collocation is the combination of words, and there are constraints. … There are conventions, there are rules, and all those that have a good vocabulary know these things” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The lecture’s activity at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>: the adjectives <em>hard</em> and <em>soft</em> go only with some nouns. Which of these words go with <em>hard</em>, which with <em>soft</em>? <em>cover, boiled, core, soap, sell, shoe, spoken, tissue, target, pedal, top, toy, copy, surface, work, drink, back, ball, court, disk, labour, line, luck</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The answers on the lecture’s slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>hard</strong>: back, ball, copy, core, court, cover, disk, labour, line, luck, surface, top, work</li>\n<li><strong>soft</strong>: ball, boiled, copy, core, cover, drink, pedal, sell, shoe, soap, spoken, target, tissue, top, toy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Five nouns take both: <em>ball, copy, core, cover, top</em>. For example, a <em>hard copy</em> is printed on paper and a <em>soft copy</em> is a computer file; a <em>soft drink</em> has no alcohol; <em>hard work</em> and <em>hard luck</em> need <em>hard</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-words-change-in-a-sentence\">How words change in a sentence</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>No word is used in isolation, except in very special settings (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). An army commander may shout one word: <em>Go! Run! Stop! Halt! Turn! Move! Shoot! Aim! Fire!</em> Even then a sentence is hidden in it: <em>Run!</em> means “You, gentlemen, please run”. In ordinary life we speak in sentences and longer talk, and we write in paragraphs.</p>\n<p>In a sentence, words often do not appear the way a dictionary lists them: their <strong>form</strong> changes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). Knowing how and why is part of knowing words.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How the words of one sentence changed (the lecture’s table at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The extract, again from <em>Swami and Friends</em>: “Six weeks later Rajam came to Swaminathan’s house to announce that he forgave him all his sins, starting with his political activities, to his new acquisition, the Board High School air, by which was meant a certain slowness and stupidity engendered by mental decay.” (<em>Acquisition</em> = something newly got; <em>engender</em> = to cause.)</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>In the text</th>\n<th>In the dictionary, and the change</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>weeks</td>\n<td>week (noun) + plural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>later</td>\n<td>late (adjective) + <em>-er</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>came</td>\n<td>come (verb), past tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Swaminathan’s</td>\n<td>Swaminathan (noun) + possessive marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>forgave</td>\n<td>forgive (verb) + past tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sins</td>\n<td>sin (noun) + plural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>starting</td>\n<td>start (verb) + progressive verb marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>political</td>\n<td>politics (noun) + adjective marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>activities</td>\n<td>activity (noun) + plural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>acquisition</td>\n<td>acquire (verb) + noun marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>meant</td>\n<td>mean (verb) + passive voice marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>slowness</td>\n<td>slow (adjective) + noun marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>stupidity</td>\n<td>stupid (adjective) + noun marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>engendered</td>\n<td>engender (verb) + passive voice marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mental</td>\n<td>mind (noun) + adjective marker</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <strong>marker</strong> is the small part that shows the new job of a word: <em>-s</em> (plural), <em>-'s</em> (possessive: who owns something), <em>-ing</em> (progressive: an action in progress), <em>-al</em> (adjective), <em>-tion, -ness, -ity</em> (noun), <em>-ly</em> (adverb). In <em>by which was meant …</em> and <em>engendered by …</em>, the verbs are in the <strong>passive voice</strong> (the doer comes after <em>by</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s notes on some of these (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Weeks</em>: after <em>six, ten, several</em> or <em>many</em>, the noun takes a plural: <em>ten weeks</em>, not <em>ten week</em>. But in <em>a 10-week guarantee period</em>, <em>10</em> and <em>week</em> together describe <em>period</em>, so there is no plural (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Late, later</em>: <em>late</em> means after the expected time. Someone expected at 10 who came at 10:30 came <strong>late</strong>; someone who came at 10:35 came <strong>later</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Political</em>: what kind of activities did Swami get into? <em>Political</em> activities. The noun <em>politics</em> became an adjective to describe the noun <em>activities</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide then sums up the changes:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Words</th>\n<th>Change in form</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>week, sin, activity</td>\n<td>singular → plural nouns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>come, forgive</td>\n<td>became past-tense verbs (<em>came, forgave</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mean, engender</td>\n<td>active voice → passive voice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>late → later</td>\n<td>change in degree</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Swaminathan</td>\n<td>no change of category: remains a noun</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"two-kinds-of-change\">Two kinds of change</h3>\n<p>There are two sorts of change (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). In one, the word keeps its category: a verb stays a verb, a noun stays a noun. In the other, the category changes: a verb becomes a noun, and so on. The lecturer calls them <strong>category-retaining</strong> and <strong>category-changing</strong> changes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). This happens in all natural languages: Arabic, Persian, Chinese, Sanskrit, Latin, Greek (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Inflection</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Inflection</strong> (the slides spell it <em>inflexion</em>) is a change of form that <strong>keeps the category</strong> (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“A verb remains a verb, though changing from present to past tense”: <em>come → came</em>, <em>forgive → forgave</em>.</li>\n<li>“Noun still remains a noun, though changing from singular to plural number”: <em>sin → sins</em>, <em>activity → activities</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What can change (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>verbs: tense, voice, aspect (<em>came</em>, <em>was meant</em>, <em>starting</em>);</li>\n<li>nouns: number (<em>weeks</em>), and the possessive (<em>Swaminathan’s</em>);</li>\n<li>adjectives and adverbs: degree (<em>late → later</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Degrees of comparison</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Positive</strong>: the plain form: <em>late</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Comparative</strong>: comparing two: <em>later</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Superlative</strong>: the most of all: <em>latest</em>, or <em>last</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Mary came late, John came later, and Simon came last</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). All three are adverbs describing <em>came</em>; only the degree changes, so this is inflection.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Derivation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Derivation</strong> is a change of form that <strong>changes the category</strong> (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>an adjective can become a noun, or</li>\n<li>a noun can become an adjective, or</li>\n<li>a verb can become a noun, or</li>\n<li>a noun can become a verb, or</li>\n<li>a noun can become an adverb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“<em>Politics</em> is a noun, but after <em>+al</em> is added to it, it becomes an adjective” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s examples of derivation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Given word</th>\n<th>Derived word</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>politics (noun)</td>\n<td>political (adjective)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>acquire (verb)</td>\n<td>acquisition (noun)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>slow (adjective)</td>\n<td>slowness (noun)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>stupid (adjective)</td>\n<td>stupidity (noun)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mind (noun)</td>\n<td>mental (adjective)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>drive (verb)</td>\n<td>driver (noun)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>person (noun)</td>\n<td>personify (verb), personal (adjective)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>slow (adjective)</td>\n<td>slowly (adverb)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nation (noun)</td>\n<td>national (adjective) → nationalize (verb) → nationalization (noun)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">One question decides it</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Ask: <strong>is it still the same part of speech?</strong> Yes → inflection (<em>day → days</em>: still a noun). No → derivation (<em>consistent → consistently</em>: adjective → adverb).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s activity, with the slide’s answers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The passage: “After making his exit from Albert Mission School in that theatrical manner (on the day following the strike), Swaminathan became so consistently stubborn that a few days later his father took him to the Board School and admitted him there. At first Swaminathan was rather uncertain of his happiness in the new school. But he excited the curiosity that all new-comers do, and found himself to his great satisfaction the centre of attraction in Second C. All his new class-mates, remarkably new faces, often clustered round him to see him and hear him talk. He had not yet picked the few that he would have liked to call his chums. He still believed that his Albert Mission set was intact, though, since the reopening in June, the set was not what it had been before.”</p>\n<p>The task: (a) which words have changed, (b) how, (c) inflection (I) or derivation (D)?</p>\n<p><strong>Inflections</strong> (the category stays):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Change</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>making</td>\n<td>make (verb) + progressive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>following</td>\n<td>follow (verb) + progressive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>reopening</td>\n<td>reopen (verb) + progressive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>became</td>\n<td>become (verb) + past tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>took</td>\n<td>take (verb) + past tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>admitted</td>\n<td>admit (verb) + past tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>excited</td>\n<td>excite (verb) + past tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>found</td>\n<td>find (verb) + past tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>believed</td>\n<td>believe (verb) + past tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>had</td>\n<td>have (verb) + past tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>been</td>\n<td>be (verb) + perfective marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>days</td>\n<td>day (noun) + plural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>chums</td>\n<td>chum (noun) + plural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>himself</td>\n<td>him (pronoun) + reflexive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Derivations</strong> (the category changes):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Change</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>theatrical</td>\n<td>theatre (noun) + adjective marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>consistently</td>\n<td>consistent (adjective) + adverb marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>happiness</td>\n<td>happy (adjective) + noun marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>curiosity</td>\n<td>curious (adjective) + noun marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>comers</td>\n<td>come (verb) + noun marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>satisfaction</td>\n<td>satisfy (verb) + noun marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>attraction</td>\n<td>attract (verb) + noun marker</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the slide lists 21 changed words: 14 inflections and 7 derivations. The passage has more, such as <em>clustered</em> and <em>faces</em> (I) and <em>remarkably</em> (D); test them the same way. (<em>Perfective</em> = the form used in <em>have been, had been</em>; <em>reflexive</em> = pointing back to the subject, as in <em>found himself</em>.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A word can change twice</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Comers</em> = <em>come</em> + <em>-er</em> (verb → noun) + <em>-s</em> (plural). The plural alone would be inflection, but the category changed from verb to noun, so the slide marks it <strong>D</strong>. Look for the category change first.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Changes follow rules</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>These changes are “pretty mechanical”: a machine can be programmed to read a text and say which words changed, how, and whether it is inflection or derivation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>). “All changes in nature, including those in languages, are regulated changes. They occur under certain principles, certain laws, certain rules” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-build-your-vocabulary\">How to build your vocabulary</h2>\n<p>The lecturer’s advice, collected from lectures 3.1 to 3.3:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nobody can build it for you.</strong> “A great vocabulary, like other aspects of language, cannot be created by others for you; each learner has to create it for themselves” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Read with one eye on the language.</strong> Give your full attention to the content, but give a little (maybe 10 or 20 percent, sometimes less) to the language: how one word goes with another, and which words the writer chose for that context (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Read and listen to good speakers and good writers</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Keep a dictionary and a thesaurus</strong> at hand, and check your answers with them.</li>\n<li><strong>Make your own exercises.</strong> Learning is best when you <em>do</em> something rather than only listen or read (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). Take any text, technical or literary (even a talk from the internet), pick a few lines, and find which words changed from their dictionary form, how, and whether it is inflection or derivation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</li>\n<li>“There is only one way to learn a language well, and that is when you take charge of learning” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"modal-verbs\">Modal verbs</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Look at <em>I can run very fast</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). <em>Run</em> is the <strong>main verb</strong>: it carries the meaning, the action. <em>Can</em> adds something else: it tells us about my <strong>ability</strong> to run. Words like <em>can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would, must</em> sit just before the main verb and colour its meaning: is the action possible, allowed, advised, required, or only likely? This colour is called <strong>modality</strong>.</p>\n<p>Notice two things. The main verb keeps its plain form: <em>run</em>, not <em>runs</em> or <em>to run</em>. And <em>can</em> has no action of its own: it is not a full verb like <em>run, go, eat, drink, play, read</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). We also see modals when asking permission (<em>May I come in?</em>) and when talking about what someone can or cannot do (<em>You can do this work; I cannot do this work</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Modal verb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>modal verb</strong>, or <strong>modal auxiliary verb</strong>, “is a class of verbs that is used to indicate modality, that is: likelihood, ability, permission, request, capacity, suggestions, order, obligation, or advice” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Modal verbs always go with the <strong>base form</strong> of another verb, the main verb, which has the meaning (the “semantic content”).</li>\n<li>A modal gives information about the function of the main verb that it governs.</li>\n<li>The modal verbs of English: <em>can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would, must, ought to, need</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Auxiliary</em> = helping: a modal is a helping verb.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Infinitive and bare infinitive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>infinitive</strong> is the basic form of a verb, often with <em>to</em>: <em>to go, to eat, to drink</em>. This <em>to</em> is the <strong>infinitive marker</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The <strong>bare infinitive</strong> is the same form without <em>to</em>: <em>go, eat, drink</em>.</li>\n<li>After a modal, the main verb is always a bare infinitive: <em>can go, will go, shall go, must go, may go</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-all-modals-share\">What all modals share</h3>\n<p>The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a> lists what modals have in common:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>no infinitive marker: the verb after them has no <em>to</em></td>\n<td><em>I can play the piano.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>questions: the modal moves to the front (inversion)</td>\n<td><em>You can play the guitar.</em> → <em>Can you play the guitar?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negatives: add <em>not</em></td>\n<td><em>cannot, will not, shall not, should not</em>; <em>You mustn’t (= must not) forget your homework.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>short forms (contractions) in speech</td>\n<td><em>can’t, couldn’t, shan’t, shouldn’t, won’t, wouldn’t, mustn’t, needn’t</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The slide adds: other contractions like <em>mightn’t</em> and <em>oughtn’t to</em> are rare, and we do <strong>not</strong> contract <em>may not</em>.</li>\n<li>In speech we use the contracted forms because they are quick and easy to say; in writing you can use the full form: <em>It cannot be true</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li>To make a question, move the modal in front: <em>I can play piano</em> → <em>Can I play piano?</em>; <em>May I come?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">After a modal, the main verb never changes</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>He can swim</em>, never <em>He can swims</em> or <em>He can to swim</em>. Whatever the subject, the main verb stays in its bare form; there is “no change in the main verb” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">English has no future form of the verb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the present we say <em>I go</em>; in the past, <em>I went</em>: the verb itself changes (inflection, as in lecture 3.3). For the future, English does not change the verb; it adds a modal: <em>I shall go</em> or <em>I will go</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-modals-one-by-one\">The modals one by one</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Can (and can’t)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ability</strong>: <em>He can swim. He can’t play tennis.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Request or permission</strong>: <em>Can I speak to Mark, please? Can you repeat that? Can I smoke?</em> (The answer: <em>you can</em>, or <em>you cannot</em> if it is not permitted.)</li>\n<li><strong>can’t = logically impossible</strong>: <em>It can’t be true. He can’t be married; he lives on his own.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer adds that <em>May I speak to Mark?</em> is already formal and polite, while with <em>can</em> you add <em>please</em> if you want to be polite (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Could</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ability in the past</strong>: <em>I could swim when I was six.</em> (Maybe I cannot now.)</li>\n<li><strong>Suggestion</strong>, when you do not want to push it: <em>We could go to the beach.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Polite request</strong>: <em>Could you tell me the time, please?</em> It is more polite than <em>Can you tell me the time?</em></li>\n<li><strong>Permission</strong>: <em>Could I leave the room? Could I finish my work?</em></li>\n<li><strong>Possibility in the present</strong> (a guess): <em>He could be French.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pairs: can/could, will/would, shall/should, may/might</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In each pair, the second word can refer to the <strong>past</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>): <em>I can swim</em> (now), <em>I could swim when I was six</em> (then). The lecturer calls this “one indication”: the second words have other jobs too, such as polite requests (<em>Could you …?</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">May</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Formal permission</strong>: <em>May I talk to you for a second?</em> A very formal, polite way to ask.</li>\n<li><strong>Things in the future that are not certain</strong>: <em>The weather may be lovely tomorrow. The train may be delayed.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Might</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A future action we are not sure about</strong>: <em>It might rain today</em> (= it is possible that it will rain). <em>I might go</em>, but I am not sure. <em>There might be an earthquake at any time.</em></li>\n<li>For such guesses, <em>may</em> and <em>might</em> are used interchangeably: <em>The train may be delayed</em> or <em>The train might be delayed</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Might</em> is also almost the <strong>past of may</strong>: when the main part of a sentence is in the past, <em>may</em> becomes <em>might</em> in the part that depends on it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). My example: <em>She says she may come</em> → <em>She said she might come.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Shall (and shan’t)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide says <em>shall</em> is used only with <em>I</em> and <em>we</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>To make or ask for a suggestion</strong>: <em>Shall we go and see a film?</em> (= Let’s go and see a film.) <em>What shall we say to him?</em></li>\n<li><strong>To offer to do something</strong>: <em>Shall I do the washing up?</em></li>\n<li><strong>To ask what someone wants you to do</strong>: <em>Where shall I put your bag?</em></li>\n<li><strong>Future</strong>, with <em>I</em> and <em>we</em>: <em>We shall overcome</em>, the famous song (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Today people use <em>will</em> more often than <em>shall</em>, even with <em>I</em> and <em>we</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). In speech, <em>I will, I shall, we will, we shall, he will, they will</em> are usually shortened to <em>I’ll, we’ll, he’ll, they’ll</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Should</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>To ask for and give advice</strong>: <em>A: What should you do if you get sunburn? B: You should put cream on your skin.</em></li>\n<li><strong>To make suggestions</strong>: <em>I think we should go home.</em></li>\n<li>One sentence can be a suggestion, advice or an order, depending on the tone and on who says it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). <em>You should take medicine on time</em> is advice from a doctor, but an order if said with strong emphasis. <em>You should come on time.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Will and would</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Will</strong> for the <strong>future</strong>, like <em>shall</em>. The lecturer gives the traditional rule: <em>will</em> with <em>you, he, she, it, they</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Will</strong> to make <strong>offers</strong>: <em>I’ll do it for you.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Will</strong> to make <strong>promises</strong>: <em>I’ll do it as soon as I can.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Would</strong>: the slide says it makes “conditional sentences in the phrase <em>Would you like …?</em>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). <em>Would you like …?</em> is a polite offer or invitation. In a conditional (<em>if</em>) sentence, <em>would</em> goes in the main part: <em>If I had time, I would come</em> (my example).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ought to</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Ought to</em> “is used in the same way as <em>should</em>”: <em>You ought to obey the law</em> = <em>You should obey the law</em>.</li>\n<li>The difference: <em>ought to</em> is <strong>prescriptive</strong> (it states what must be done, like a rule); <em>should</em> is the normal word for a suggestion or advice (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n<li>In the negative, you usually say <em>shouldn’t</em>, not <em>oughtn’t to</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Ought to</em> is one unit</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <em>to</em> of <em>ought to</em> belongs to the modal. It is not an infinitive marker, and the main verb after it is still bare (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). So <em>you ought to go</em>, but never <em>you should to go</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Need</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Usually a <strong>normal verb</strong>, with normal questions and negatives: <em>Do you need anything? I don’t need to do anything.</em></li>\n<li>It also works as a modal, mostly in the negative <strong>needn’t</strong>, which is very common: <em>We needn’t go immediately.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Must and mustn’t</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>To express an obligation or give an order</strong>: <em>You must go to bed early. You must stop doing that. We must be more careful in future.</em></li>\n<li><strong>To give advice</strong>, in a very strong way: <em>If you go to Britain, you must try fish and chips.</em></li>\n<li><em>Must</em> adds a high degree of certainty and emphasis: the action becomes almost <strong>mandatory</strong> (compulsory).</li>\n<li><strong>mustn’t</strong> expresses a <strong>prohibition</strong> (an order <em>not</em> to do something): <em>You mustn’t go swimming after lunch.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Modal</th>\n<th>Main uses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>can</td>\n<td>ability; request, permission; <em>can’t</em> = impossible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>could</td>\n<td>past ability; polite request; permission; suggestion; a guess about now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>may</td>\n<td>formal permission; an uncertain future</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>might</td>\n<td>an uncertain future; the past of <em>may</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>shall</td>\n<td>suggestions, offers, future (with <em>I, we</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>should</td>\n<td>advice; suggestions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>will</td>\n<td>future; offers; promises</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>would</td>\n<td>polite offers: <em>Would you like …?</em>; conditionals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>must</td>\n<td>obligation, order, strong advice; <em>mustn’t</em> = prohibition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ought to</td>\n<td>like <em>should</em>, but prescriptive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>need</td>\n<td>mostly <em>needn’t</em> = it is not necessary</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"phrases-that-work-like-modals\">Phrases that work like modals</h3>\n<p>Some groups of words behave like modal verbs (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Have to and have got to</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>have to</strong> is used in place of <em>must</em> to express an <strong>obligation</strong>: <em>New students have to / must register on their first day.</em></li>\n<li>It is also used for the <strong>past or future</strong>, because <em>must</em> has no past or future form: <em>We all had to give in our names. You will have to be brave.</em></li>\n<li><strong>have got to</strong> is used in the same way, but it is more <strong>colloquial</strong> (everyday spoken English), used for an obligation at the moment of speaking. The negative is <em>haven’t got to</em>; the question, <em>Have you got to …?</em> Examples: <em>I’ve got to go to the bank. We haven’t got to be there until 12 o’clock. Have you got to change money?</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1452s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:12</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Don’t have to (doesn’t have to)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Something is <strong>not necessary</strong>, not obligatory: <em>British men don’t have to do military service.</em> The lecturer adds that in India too, military service is not compulsory (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Must or have to?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>They mean nearly the same but are used differently (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1504s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:04</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>must</strong>: the <strong>speaker</strong> makes the obligation. <em>“You must do your homework,” said the teacher.</em> It is the teacher who says so.</li>\n<li><strong>have to</strong>: an <strong>organisation</strong>, such as a school or a government, imposes the obligation. <em>You have to have a visa to visit the USA. In that school they have to do a lot of homework.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Mustn’t or don’t have to?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“These two forms mean different things” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1576s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:16</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>mustn’t</strong> = an obligation <strong>not</strong> to do something. It is like <em>can’t</em>: <em>You mustn’t smoke in here</em> (= You can’t smoke in here).</li>\n<li><strong>don’t have to</strong> = <strong>no obligation</strong> at all: you can do what you like. It is like <em>needn’t</em> or <em>don’t need to</em>: <em>You don’t have to come</em> (= You don’t need to come).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Be able to and be allowed to</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>be able to</strong> is used like <em>can</em>, but only for <strong>ability</strong>: <em>He is able to walk now. I am not able to catch up with you today.</em></li>\n<li><strong>be allowed to</strong> is used in place of <em>can</em> or <em>could</em> for <strong>permission</strong>: <em>Are we allowed to smoke? We weren’t allowed to go out after nine at night.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Be</em> changes with the subject and the tense: <em>am, is, are, was, were</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Used to + verb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>For states, situations or repeated actions (habits and routines) that you did <strong>in the past</strong> but don’t do now (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Repeated action</strong>: <em>She used to play tennis every week, but now she plays football.</em></li>\n<li><strong>State</strong>: <em>I used to be very fat, but I’ve lost a lot of weight.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Situation</strong>: <em>I used to live in Brazil.</em> (The lecturer adds Chennai, Delhi, England, America.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You cannot use this form in the present: it is only for the past (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Used to</em> (a past habit) or <em>be used to</em> (accustomed)?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide warns not to confuse the verb <em>used to</em> + verb with the adjective <em>used to</em> + noun or <em>-ing</em> form, which means “accustomed to” or “familiar with”. My examples: <em>I used to wake up at 5</em> (I did then, not now). <em>I am used to waking up at 5</em> (it is normal for me).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Meaning in the main verb, modality in the modal</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>can go, will go, shall go, must go, may go</em>, the word <em>go</em> carries the meaning; <em>can, will, shall, must, may</em> add the modality (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). Modal + bare infinitive, every time.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Contractions are not only for modals</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Can’t, won’t, mustn’t</em> are contractions of modals, but other words contract too: <em>don’t</em> and <em>doesn’t</em> (in <em>don’t have to</em>) are contractions of <em>do</em>, which is not a modal, and <em>I’ve got to</em> contracts <em>have</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"phrases-and-idioms\">Phrases and idioms</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lectures 3.5 and 3.6 teach expressions that make our conversation “more expressive and impressive” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). Many of them do not mean what their words say: <em>kill two birds with one stone</em> asks you to kill no birds at all. “Never kill a bird; do not take that literally” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Phrase</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“In common usage, a <strong>phrase</strong> is usually a group of words with some special idiomatic meaning or other significance, such as <em>all rights reserved</em>, <em>economical with the truth</em>, <em>kick the bucket</em>, and the like. It may be a euphemism, a saying or proverb, a fixed expression, a figure of speech, etc.” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>all rights reserved</em>: a fixed expression printed in books and on websites: the owner keeps all the rights.</li>\n<li><em>economical with the truth</em>: a polite way of saying someone is not telling the whole truth.</li>\n<li><em>kick the bucket</em>: to die.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Euphemism</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>euphemism</strong> is “a polite word or expression that is used to refer to things which people may find upsetting or embarrassing to talk about, for example sex, the human body, or death” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). <em>Kick the bucket</em> = die; <em>make love</em> = have sex.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Proverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>proverb</strong> is “a simple, concrete, traditional saying that expresses a truth based on common sense or experience” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). <em>An apple a day keeps the doctor away</em>: an apple a day, with its nutrition, keeps you fit and healthy, so you do not need a doctor.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Saying</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>saying</strong> is “any concisely written or spoken expression that is especially memorable because of its meaning or style” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Idiom</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>idiom</strong> is “a phrase, saying or a group of words that has a metaphorical (not literal) meaning, which has become accepted in common usage”. Its symbolic sense “is quite different from the literal meaning or definition of the words of which it is made” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Literal</strong> meaning: the plain meaning of each word. <strong>Metaphorical</strong> meaning: a meaning that comes from a picture or a comparison.</li>\n<li><em>kick the bucket</em> = to die. <em>break a leg</em> does not mean breaking anyone’s leg; it wishes someone good luck: <em>Hey chap, break a leg!</em> = Hey chap, good luck! (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Learn each idiom as one block</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Learn the whole expression, its meaning and one example sentence together, and never translate it word by word. The idiom is fixed: change one word and it sounds wrong.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"common-phrases-from-lecture-3-5\">Common phrases from lecture 3.5</h2>\n<p>Each phrase below has the slide’s meaning and the lecture’s example sentence.</p>\n<h3 id=\"phrases-1-to-10\">Phrases 1 to 10</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>a dark horse</strong>: an unexpected winner. <em>The movie is a dark horse for the award.</em> (Nobody expected it to win.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>bite the bullet</strong>: force yourself to do something unpleasant or difficult; be brave in a difficult situation. <em>Shweta has to learn to bite the bullet and face her fear of the dark.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>break a leg</strong>: good luck (said before a performance). <em>Oh, this is your first audition, isn’t it? Break a leg, man!</em> (An <em>audition</em> is a short performance to try for a role.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>make a mountain out of a molehill</strong>: exaggerate a minor difficulty; make a big fuss about something small. <em>She just asked you to lower your pitch; it is nothing to make a mountain out of a molehill.</em> (The lecturer builds it from <em>it is nothing to make a fuss about</em>; the natural form is <em>don’t make a mountain out of a molehill</em>.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>kill two birds with one stone</strong>: achieve two aims with a single effort. <em>Whenever I go jogging, I listen to English audiobooks. In that way I kill two birds with one stone: I stay fit, and I also improve my English.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>move heaven and earth</strong>: make the maximum effort. <em>His friends had to move heaven and earth to bring him out of prison.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>keep the ball rolling</strong>: continue the work. <em>I have started the preparations for the party, but it is up to you to keep the ball rolling.</em> (I did my part; you continue.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>be in the driving seat</strong>: bear all the responsibilities; be in control. <em>At 70, he is as firmly in the driving seat of the company as ever.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>out of my league</strong>: the other person is superior, better, at a higher level. <em>I won’t propose to her; she is the most beautiful, rich and intelligent girl I have ever met. She is out of my league.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>a blessing in disguise</strong>: a good thing that seemed bad at first. <em>My car broke down again, but maybe it was a blessing in disguise, because I have been wasting too much time and money driving around anyway.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"phrases-11-to-20\">Phrases 11 to 20</h3>\n<ol start=\"11\">\n<li><strong>a piece of cake</strong>: very easy. <em>Ram found the project to be a piece of cake because it was incredibly easy.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>money burns a hole in your pocket</strong>: you spend money very quickly. <em>He can’t hold on to money; it always burns a hole in his pocket.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>cut your coat according to your cloth</strong>: live within your means (spend only what you can afford). <em>We would like a bigger house, but we must cut our coat according to our cloth.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>once in a blue moon</strong>: rarely. <em>I do not know why she bought that car; she uses it once in a blue moon.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>put (something) in cold storage</strong>: keep work pending, on hold. <em>We can’t consider these design changes now; let us put them in cold storage for a year or so.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>look for a needle in a haystack</strong>: look for something that is almost impossible to find. <em>We have been looking for an apartment here for a month now, and it is like finding a needle in a haystack.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>miss the boat</strong>: miss the opportunity to do something. <em>The discounted price sale ended today, and I just missed the boat on making a great deal.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>pull yourself together</strong>: calm down and act normally again. <em>Roy is finding it difficult to pull himself together after that accident.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>hear (something) on the grapevine</strong>: hear a rumour, an unconfirmed story, from other people rather than an official source. <em>I heard on the grapevine that Jahan and Rumaisa are getting married.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>cut corners</strong>: do something in the fastest and cheapest way (skipping proper steps). <em>When the boss found that the accounting department was cutting corners, he fired them all.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"phrases-21-to-30\">Phrases 21 to 30</h3>\n<ol start=\"21\">\n<li><strong>between the devil and the deep sea</strong>: between two difficult situations. <em>He is caught between the devil and the deep sea when his wife and his mother fight.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>beat around the bush</strong>: avoid saying what you mean, usually because it is uncomfortable. <em>Do not beat around the bush; state your case in simple and direct words.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>better late than never</strong>: it is better to arrive or do something later than expected than not to do it at all. <em>It took me a long time to learn, but better late than never.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>out of order</strong>: not working properly. <em>The elevator is out of order again.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>out of place</strong>: looking or feeling different from the other people or things in a place. <em>The velvet sofa is out of place on the porch.</em> (It does not fit there.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>flog a dead horse</strong>: waste your effort on something that is already finished. <em>We have all moved on from that problem, so there is no use flogging a dead horse.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>actions speak louder than words</strong>: what someone actually does means more than what they say they will do. <em>Do not tell me how to do this; show me. Actions speak louder than words.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>bite off more than you can chew</strong>: try to do something that is too difficult or too much for you. <em>By accepting two part-time jobs, he is clearly biting off more than he can chew.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>out of character</strong>: behaving differently from usual. <em>Ann’s remark was quite out of character; it was out of character for Ann to act so stubbornly.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>get a taste of your own medicine</strong>: be treated the way you have been treating other people. <em>I do not feel sorry that people are calling you names; you are getting a taste of your own medicine.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-aTwRZVfo64&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">See the picture (these pictures are not from the lecture)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a dark horse</strong>: in a horse race, a horse that nobody knows much about, which then wins.</li>\n<li><strong>a molehill</strong>: the small heap of earth that a mole (a small digging animal) pushes up. Calling it a mountain is a huge exaggeration.</li>\n<li><strong>cut your coat according to your cloth</strong>: a tailor can only make a coat as big as the cloth allows.</li>\n<li><strong>a haystack</strong>: a huge pile of dried grass; a needle lost in it is almost impossible to find.</li>\n<li><strong>miss the boat</strong>: you reach the jetty after the boat has left.</li>\n<li><strong>flog a dead horse</strong>: beating a dead horse will never make it run.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Say them exactly</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides write some phrases in short form; use the full fixed form:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>bite <strong>the</strong> bullet</em> (the slide says “bite a bullet”; the lecture’s sentence uses <em>the</em>).</li>\n<li><em>kill two birds with <strong>one</strong> stone</em>.</li>\n<li><em>cut <strong>your</strong> coat according to <strong>your</strong> cloth</em> (or <em>our … our</em>, <em>his … his</em>: the same owner both times).</li>\n<li><em>hear something <strong>on</strong> the grapevine</em> (also <em>through the grapevine</em>).</li>\n<li><em>between the devil and the deep sea</em> (also <em>the deep blue sea</em>).</li>\n<li><em>beat <strong>around</strong> the bush</em> (in British English, <em>beat about the bush</em>).</li>\n<li><em><strong>Actions</strong> speak louder than words</em> (plural).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"common-phrases-from-lecture-3-6\">Common phrases from lecture 3.6</h2>\n<h3 id=\"phrases-31-to-40\">Phrases 31 to 40</h3>\n<ol start=\"31\">\n<li><strong>add insult to injury</strong>: act in a way that makes a bad situation worse. <em>I was already getting late for work, stuck in the traffic, and to add insult to injury, I was stopped by the police for over-speeding.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>at the eleventh hour</strong>: at the last moment. <em>Just minutes before the deadline, he submitted his assignment at the eleventh hour.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>out of shape</strong>: not physically strong or fit; not in a healthy condition. <em>I am tired the whole day. Oh man, I think I am getting out of shape.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>out of the loop</strong>: uninformed; not having the information that everyone else has. <em>She may be out of the loop for a short time.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>the apple of your eye</strong>: a person you love very much (the slide says “lovable”). <em>I love my niece Shreya; she is the apple of my eye.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>crocodile tears</strong>: false tears; pretending to be sad. <em>The sight of David shedding crocodile tears made me sick.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>hold your tongue</strong>: remain silent. <em>If you do not hold your tongue, you will have to walk out of the class.</em> (A teacher to a student who is talking back.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>a white elephant</strong>: a costly but useless possession. <em>The bridge remains underused and has become a white elephant project.</em> (It was very expensive to build, and people do not use it.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>out of touch</strong>: not communicating with each other, for a long time. <em>I have been out of touch with my cousin for many years.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>bark up the wrong tree</strong>: be wrong about the way to achieve something, or about the reason for something. <em>I have been trying to solve this maths question, but I think I have been barking up the wrong tree.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"phrases-41-to-50\">Phrases 41 to 50</h3>\n<ol start=\"41\">\n<li><strong>call it a day</strong>: stop what you are doing because you think you have done enough, or you do not want to do it any more. <em>I am getting a bit tired now; let us call it a day.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>take the law into your own hands</strong>: punish someone according to your own idea of justice. <em>After his daughter’s killer was let off without a conviction, the man decided to take the law into his own hands.</em> (A <em>conviction</em> is a court’s decision that someone is guilty.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>take the bull by the horns</strong>: face difficulties in a direct way, especially difficult and dangerous ones. <em>The government will have to take the bull by the horns and tackle the problem of inflation.</em> (<em>Inflation</em> = rising prices.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>burn the candle at both ends</strong>: work extremely hard. <em>Ever since I started my new job, I have been burning the candle at both ends.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>break the ice</strong>: make people who have not met before feel more relaxed and comfortable. <em>He organised a few party games to break the ice when people first arrived.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>a nine days’ wonder</strong>: something short-lived: people talk about it for a few days, then forget it. <em>Do not worry about the story in the newspaper; it will be a nine days’ wonder, and then people will forget it.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>the ball is in your court</strong>: it is your turn to take the next step or decision. <em>Dev made the last offer; the ball is in Shiv’s court now.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>cost an arm and a leg</strong>: be very expensive. <em>Is it really worth spending an arm and a leg on certain types of cosmetics?</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>put the cart before the horse</strong>: do things wrongly, in the wrong order. <em>I think you are putting the cart before the horse by leaving your permanent job before getting a new one.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>get your act together</strong>: organise yourself so that you can do things effectively. <em>I wish she could get her act together.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"phrases-51-to-60\">Phrases 51 to 60</h3>\n<ol start=\"51\">\n<li><strong>put all your eggs in one basket</strong>: depend for success on a single person or plan. <em>His financial adviser urged him to be careful and not put all his eggs in one basket by investing all his money in stocks.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>every cloud has a silver lining</strong>: every negative has a positive. <em>Do not worry about breaking your leg; you will be able to relax all day on the sofa watching your favourite TV shows. So you see, every cloud has a silver lining.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>get out of hand</strong>: become difficult to control. <em>Please call for help; I do not want the fire to get out of hand.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>cry wolf</strong>: call for help when you do not need it. <em>Do not pay attention to Sitara; she is only crying wolf.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>it takes two to tango</strong>: an action or a communication needs more than one person, so both share the responsibility. <em>Do not blame me for the argument; it takes two to tango. You are equally responsible.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>get (something) out of your system</strong>: get rid of a wish or an emotion, especially a negative one, by allowing yourself to express it. <em>Please make a diary entry of all the negative emotions you feel; you will feel better if you get it out of your system.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>sit (or be) on the fence</strong>: be undecided; take a neutral stand; not take sides. At an ice-cream counter you cannot choose between chocolate and vanilla, and the seller says: <em>You cannot sit on the fence for long; make your decision fast.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>step up your game</strong>: start performing better. <em>You really need to step up your game if you want to stay in this company.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>sell someone out</strong>: snitch on someone (tell others what they did) or let their secret out. <em>The company had put a lot of trust in him, but he sold them out by leaking confidential information to the competitors. Can I trust you on this, or will you sell me out?</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>be all Greek to someone</strong>: not be understood at all. <em>I tried reading that science journal, but it was all Greek to me.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r5wn7XuDCV8&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">See the picture (these pictures are not from the lecture)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>crocodile tears</strong>: from an old belief that crocodiles shed tears while they eat their prey.</li>\n<li><strong>a white elephant</strong>: in an old story, a king gave a white elephant to someone he disliked: the animal was holy, so it could not be put to work, and feeding it cost a fortune.</li>\n<li><strong>cry wolf</strong>: from the old fable of the shepherd boy who shouted “Wolf!” as a joke; when a wolf really came, nobody believed him.</li>\n<li><strong>burn the candle at both ends</strong>: a candle lit at both ends burns out twice as fast.</li>\n<li><strong>put the cart before the horse</strong>: the horse must go in front to pull the cart.</li>\n<li><strong>at the eleventh hour</strong>: on a clock face, only one hour is left before twelve.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Say them exactly</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>the apple of my <strong>eye</strong></em> (singular <em>eye</em>; the slide writes “eyes”).</li>\n<li><em>take the law into your <strong>own hands</strong></em>; <em>take the bull by <strong>the</strong> horns</em>; <em>a nine <strong>days’</strong> wonder</em>.</li>\n<li><em>put <strong>all</strong> your eggs in one basket</em>, usually in the negative: <em>Don’t put all your eggs in one basket.</em></li>\n<li><em>It’s all Greek to me.</em></li>\n<li><em>cost an arm and a leg</em>; the lecture’s sentence uses <em>spend an arm and a leg</em>. After <em>worth</em>, use the <em>-ing</em> form: <em>worth spending</em>, not <em>worth to spend</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"phrases-that-look-alike\">Phrases that look alike</h2>\n<p>Ten of the sixty contain <em>out of</em>. Leaving aside <em>make a mountain out of a molehill</em>, do not mix up these nine:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>out of my league</td>\n<td>the other person is better, at a higher level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>out of order</td>\n<td>not working properly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>out of place</td>\n<td>different from the people or things around</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>out of character</td>\n<td>behaving differently from usual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>out of shape</td>\n<td>not physically fit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>out of the loop</td>\n<td>not having the information everyone else has</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>out of touch</td>\n<td>not in contact for a long time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>get out of hand</td>\n<td>become difficult to control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>get something out of your system</td>\n<td>get rid of a feeling by expressing it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember them in families</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Horses</strong>: <em>a dark horse</em> (an unexpected winner), <em>flog a dead horse</em> (waste your effort), <em>put the cart before the horse</em> (do things in the wrong order).</li>\n<li><strong>Balls</strong>: <em>keep the ball rolling</em> (continue the work), <em>the ball is in your court</em> (your turn to decide).</li>\n<li><strong>Eating</strong>: <em>a piece of cake</em> (very easy), <em>bite off more than you can chew</em> (take on too much), <em>a taste of your own medicine</em> (treated as you treat others), <em>bite the bullet</em> (face something unpleasant).</li>\n<li><strong>Body</strong>: <em>break a leg</em> (good luck), <em>cost an arm and a leg</em> (very expensive), <em>hold your tongue</em> (stay silent), <em>the apple of my eye</em> (a dear one).</li>\n<li><strong>Time</strong>: <em>at the eleventh hour</em> (at the last moment), <em>once in a blue moon</em> (rarely), <em>better late than never</em>, <em>a nine days’ wonder</em> (short-lived), <em>call it a day</em> (stop for now).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vocabulary</strong> = the words you know + all you know about them: meaning, spelling, pronunciation, part of speech, forms, and which words they go with. No one knows the exact number (college graduate: about 30,000–50,000).</li>\n<li><strong>Five signs of a good vocabulary</strong>: number and variety of words; similar and opposite words; how each word goes with others; how new words are made from old ones; how each word is used in a sentence.</li>\n<li><strong>Synonyms</strong>: almost the same meaning (<em>road, avenue, lane, street</em>), never exactly alike. <strong>Antonyms</strong>: opposite meaning (<em>friend, enemy</em>). Only <em>friends</em> and <em>enemies</em> = a functional vocabulary; their synonyms and antonyms too = a good one.</li>\n<li><strong>Dictionary</strong>: alphabetical; spelling, meaning, category, usage, pronunciation. <strong>Thesaurus</strong>: words grouped by meaning.</li>\n<li><strong>Homophones</strong>: same sound, different spelling and meaning (<em>right, write</em>). <strong>Homonyms</strong>: same spelling and sound, different meanings (<em>beat</em>: to hit; a policeman’s area).</li>\n<li><strong>Collocation</strong>: the fixed relationship of which words go together. <em>many, few</em> + countable nouns; <em>much, little</em> + uncountable nouns; <em>electric guitar</em>, <em>electrical engineer</em>; <em>drink</em> liquids, <em>eat</em> solids.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflection</strong>: the form changes, the category stays (<em>week → weeks</em>, <em>come → came</em>, <em>late → later</em>). <strong>Derivation</strong>: the category changes (<em>politics → political</em>, <em>acquire → acquisition</em>, <em>slow → slowly</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Modal verbs</strong>: <em>can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would, must, ought to, need</em>. Modal + bare infinitive (<em>can go</em>); questions put the modal first (<em>Can you …?</em>); negatives add <em>not</em>; contractions <em>can’t, won’t, mustn’t</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Uses</strong>: <em>can</em> ability, request, <em>can’t</em> = impossible; <em>could</em> past ability, polite request, suggestion, a guess; <em>may</em> formal permission, uncertain future; <em>might</em> uncertain future; <em>shall</em> suggestions and offers with <em>I, we</em>; <em>should</em> advice; <em>will</em> future, offers, promises; <em>must</em> obligation, strong advice; <em>mustn’t</em> prohibition; <em>ought to</em> = <em>should</em>, prescriptive; <em>needn’t</em> = not necessary.</li>\n<li><strong>Like modals</strong>: <em>have to</em> (obligation from outside; also past and future), <em>have got to</em> (colloquial), <em>don’t have to</em> (no obligation, unlike <em>mustn’t</em>), <em>be able to</em> (ability), <em>be allowed to</em> (permission), <em>used to</em> (past habits and states only).</li>\n<li><strong>Phrase</strong>: a group of words with a special meaning: a <strong>euphemism</strong> (a polite word for something upsetting), a <strong>proverb</strong> (a traditional truth), a <strong>saying</strong> (a memorable expression), or an <strong>idiom</strong> (a metaphorical, not literal, meaning).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Inflection or derivation? <em>days</em>, <em>became</em>, <em>happiness</em>, <em>consistently</em> (words from the lecture’s passage)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Ask whether the part of speech changed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>days</em> = <em>day</em> (noun) + plural → still a noun → <strong>inflection</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>became</em> = <em>become</em> (verb) + past tense → still a verb → <strong>inflection</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>happiness</em> = <em>happy</em> (adjective) + noun marker <em>-ness</em> → now a noun → <strong>derivation</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>consistently</em> = <em>consistent</em> (adjective) + adverb marker <em>-ly</em> → now an adverb → <strong>derivation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Choose <em>much</em>, <em>many</em>, <em>few</em> or <em>little</em>: (a) Chennai has ___ water for so many people. (b) There were ___ students in the hall, so it was full. (c) Don’t put ___ sugar in my tea. (d) ___ girls came, so the class was almost empty.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>First ask: can the noun be counted?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>little</strong>: <em>water</em> is uncountable, and there is hardly any (the lecture’s sentence).</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>many</strong>: <em>students</em> can be counted; a large number.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>much</strong>: <em>sugar</em> is uncountable; a large quantity (sentences (b) to (d) are mine).</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Few</strong>: <em>girls</em> can be counted; a small number.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which modal fits? (a) ___ you tell me the time, please? (a polite request) (b) I ___ swim when I was six. (c) ___ we go and see a film? (a suggestion) (d) You ___ smoke in here. (it is not allowed) (e) You ___ come; it is up to you.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Could</strong>: a polite request (<em>can</em> is possible, but <em>could</em> is more polite).</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>could</strong>: ability in the past.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Shall</strong>: a suggestion with <em>we</em> (= Let’s go and see a film).</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>mustn’t</strong>: a prohibition, an obligation <em>not</em> to do it (= You can’t smoke in here).</li>\n<li>(e) <strong>don’t have to</strong> (or <strong>needn’t</strong>): no obligation; you can do what you like.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All five sentences are from the lecture’s slides.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What do the idioms mean? (a) <em>Dev made the last offer; the ball is in Shiv’s court now.</em> (b) <em>I heard on the grapevine that Jahan and Rumaisa are getting married.</em> (c) <em>By accepting two part-time jobs, he is clearly biting off more than he can chew.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Dev has done his part; now it is <strong>Shiv’s turn</strong> to take the next step or decision.</li>\n<li>(b) The speaker heard a <strong>rumour</strong>, an unconfirmed story from other people, not official news.</li>\n<li>(c) He has taken on <strong>too much</strong>: two jobs are more than he can handle.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which phrase means: (a) rarely (b) false tears (c) to exaggerate a minor difficulty (d) a costly but useless possession (e) at the last moment?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>once in a blue moon</strong></li>\n<li>(b) <strong>crocodile tears</strong></li>\n<li>(c) <strong>make a mountain out of a molehill</strong></li>\n<li>(d) <strong>a white elephant</strong></li>\n<li>(e) <strong>at the eleventh hour</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Euphemism, proverb or idiom? (a) <em>kick the bucket</em> (b) <em>An apple a day keeps the doctor away.</em> (c) <em>break a leg</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A <strong>euphemism</strong>: a polite way to speak about death. It is also an idiom, because its meaning (to die) is not the literal one.</li>\n<li>(b) A <strong>proverb</strong>: a simple, traditional saying that states a truth from common sense and experience.</li>\n<li>(c) An <strong>idiom</strong>: its metaphorical meaning, “good luck”, is quite different from the literal meaning of the words.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n"});
